\section{Shape Is Decided Before Simulation Time}

Elaboration converts already analyzed design units into a specific design instance. It resolves constants, binds instances, expands generate constructs, and determines the types and widths of their connections. Simulation begins with that design and advances signals through time. Synthesis reduces and maps the design for a target. A tool can combine these operations internally, but the concepts are distinct: one can inspect an elaborated design without knowing which physical cells will implement it.

Himmelbauer and colleagues' \emph{The Vienna Architecture Description Language} distinguishes reusable specification mechanisms and generated deliverables, including synthesizable HDL \cite{himmelbauer2024vadl}. An upstream generator can emit a whole HDL design with a chosen microarchitecture, but it can also leave parameters for the downstream HDL elaborator. Changing such a parameter is a new build of the hardware, not a command issued to a running instance.

Parameters therefore describe a family of designs. A port declares what a member of that family can receive or produce while it operates. Documentation belongs at both levels: for a parameter, specify its permitted values and what changes with it; for a port, describe its protocol, timing, and validity. A local parameter can express an invariant derived inside the module, whereas a public parameter invites the instantiating design to choose a value. Treat publicly supported configurations as separate verification cases rather than assuming that the defaults stand for all of them.

\section{Generate a Wire Path or a Register Bank}

These SystemVerilog modules (IEEE 1800-2023) use a constant conditional generate and a generate loop \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}. They are simulatable and use ordinary synthesizable RTL. The design contract is \verb|W >= 1|; SystemVerilog packed ranges with reversed bounds do not represent an empty vector. Neither module specifies a reset or a defined power-up value for the registered taps.

\begin{verbatim}
module tap #(
    parameter int W = 8,
    parameter bit REGISTERED = 1'b0
) (
    input  wire         clk,
    input  wire [W-1:0] d,
    output wire [W-1:0] q
);
    if (REGISTERED) begin : g_reg
        for (genvar i = 0; i < W; i++) begin : g_lane
            logic sample;
            always_ff @(posedge clk) sample <= d[i];
            assign q[i] = sample;
        end
    end else begin : g_direct
        assign q = d;
    end
endmodule

module two_taps (
    input  wire       clk,
    input  wire [2:0] d3,
    input  wire [4:0] d5,
    output wire [2:0] q3,
    output wire [4:0] q5
);
    tap #(.W(3)) u_direct (.clk(clk), .d(d3), .q(q3));
    tap #(.W(5), .REGISTERED(1'b1))
        u_reg (.clk(clk), .d(d5), .q(q5));
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

The unselected branch contributes neither a driver nor any structure. Each iteration of \verb|g_lane| introduces a scoped declaration and process; for example, its highest index in the five-bit specialization is \verb|u_reg.g_reg.g_lane[4].sample|. The index is fixed for that instance. No register increments it at run time. The unused \texttt{clk} port of the direct tap remains part of its declared interface despite having no effect on its result.

